1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,875
How Does a Microwave Heat Food?

2
00:00:02,825 --> 00:00:11,887
A microwave oven generates electromagnetic radiation at 2.45 gigahertz, corresponding to a wavelength of 12.2 centimeters.

3
00:00:11,887 --> 00:00:22,700
This frequency was selected because it lies within the industrial, scientific, and medical (ISM) band allocated internationally to avoid interference with telecommunications.

4
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:32,487
At this frequency, the alternating electric field causes dipolar molecules — primarily water — to rotate at 2.45 billion cycles per second.

5
00:00:32,487 --> 00:00:39,862
The rotating molecules collide with neighboring molecules, transferring kinetic energy as heat throughout the volume of the food.

6
00:00:39,862 --> 00:00:48,950
This dielectric heating differs fundamentally from conventional ovens, which transfer heat via infrared radiation and convection from the surface inward.

7
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:59,250
Penetration depth in typical food ranges from one to several centimeters, meaning thick items cook from the outside in and the interior continues cooking via thermal conduction.

8
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:06,462
Microwave ovens contain a magnetron — a vacuum tube that converts electrical power into microwave radiation.

9
00:01:06,462 --> 00:01:18,412
The metal walls of the cooking cavity form a Faraday cage that reflects microwaves back into the food; the metal mesh in the door window has holes smaller than the wavelength to contain radiation while permitting visible light.

10
00:01:18,412 --> 00:01:24,862
A rotating mode stirrer or turntable distributes the standing wave pattern to reduce hot and cold spots.

11
00:01:24,862 --> 00:01:37,075
The microwave oven was discovered accidentally in 1945 when Percy Spencer, an engineer at Raytheon, noticed a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted while standing near an active radar magnetron.

12
00:01:37,075 --> 00:01:44,175
He subsequently tested the effect on popcorn and an egg, and Raytheon filed a patent in 1945.

13
00:01:44,175 --> 00:01:53,662
The first commercial model, the Radarange, was released in 1947, standing 1.8 meters tall and weighing 340 kilograms.

